kind":"ruler","value_qid":"Q57146","value_label":"Carol I of Romania","from_t":1866.2986301369863,"to_t":1881.2,"title":"Prince of Romania","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Carol I of Romania"},{"qid":"Q218","kind":"flag","value_qid":"Q130811","value_label":"Flag of Romania.svg","from_t":1848.483606557377,"to_t":null,"title":"flag of Romania","property":"P163","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"flag of Romania"}],"people":{"Q11163124":{"label":"Nicușor Dan","description":"President of Romania since 2025","enwiki":"Nicușor Dan","summary":"Nicușor Daniel Dan (born 20 December 1969) is a Romanian mathematician and politician serving as the seventh President of Romania since 2025. He previously served as the Mayor of Bucharest from 2020 to 2025 and as a member of the Chamber of Deputies from 2016 to 2020.\nBorn in Făgăraș, Brașov County, Dan studied mathematics at the University of Bucharest before continuing his studies in France, where he obtained a Master's degree from the École normale supérieure and a PhD from Paris 13 University. After returning to Romania, he founded the Școala Normală Superioară București, a research institution in mathematics, and participated in civic initiatives concerning urban governance and heritage preservation.","image":"Nicusor Dan in 2026.jpg","birth":1969.9671232876713,"death":null},"Q121140873":{"label":"Luca Niculescu","description":"Romanian journalist and diplomat","enwiki":"Luca Niculescu","summary":"Luca Alexandru Niculescu (born 25 April 1971) is a Romanian journalist and diplomat who has been serving as the Prime Minister of Romania since 20 October 2026. He previously worked as a journalist in Romanian and French media outlets for several years. Niculescu was the ambassador of Romania to France, Andorra and Monaco from 2016 to 2022. From 2022 to 2026, he served as secretary of state of the Ministry of Foreign Affairs of Romania for the country's accession to the Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development (OECD).","image":"First press conference of PM Luca Niculescu (cropped).jpg","birth":1971.3123287671233,"death":null},"Q18538164":{"label":"Ilie Bolojan","description":"Romanian politician (born in 1969)","enwiki":"Ilie Bolojan","summary":"Ilie Gavril Bolojan (born 17 March 1969) is a Romanian politician who served as Prime Minister of Romania from 2025 to 2026. Previously, he served as the acting president of Romania in 2025, in his capacity as President of the Senate from 2024 to 2025. \nBolojan previously served as Mayor of Oradea and President of the Bihor County Council. Following the National Liberal Party's defeat in the first round of the later annulled 2024 Romanian presidential election, and the subsequent resignation of Nicolae Ciucă, Bolojan became the party's acting president and was elected president of the Senate of Romania.","image":"Ilie Bolojan (16 April 2026) (cropped).jpg","birth":1969.2054794520548,"death":null},"Q189575":{"label":"Ion Iliescu","description":"President of Romania between 1990–1996 and 2000–2004","enwiki":"Ion Iliescu","summary":"Ion Iliescu (3 March 1930 – 5 August 2025) was a Romanian politician and engineer who served as the first and third President of Romania since the country's transition to democracy, from 1990 to 1996 and 2000 to 2004. Iliescu was also a senator for the Social Democratic Party (PSD), which he founded and where he became the honorary president for the rest of his life.\nIliescu joined the Romanian Communist Party (PCR) in 1953 and became a member of its Central Committee in 1965. Beginning with 1971, he was gradually marginalised by Nicolae Ceaușescu.","image":"Ion Iliescu (2004).jpg","birth":1930.1671232876713,"death":2025.5917808219178},"Q299152":{"label":"Emil Constantinescu","description":"President of Romania from 1996 to 2000","enwiki":"Emil Constantinescu","summary":"Emil Constantinescu (born 19 November 1939) is a Romanian politician and professor, who served as the second President of Romania from 1996 to 2000. After the Romanian Revolution of 1989, Constantinescu became a founding member and vice president of the Civic Alliance (AC). In addition, he also served as the acting president of the Democratic Romanian Anti-Totalitarian Forum, the first associative structure of the democratic opposition in post-1989 Romania, which was later transformed into a centre-right political and electoral alliance known as the Romanian Democratic Convention (CDR). He subsequently presided over the People's Action (AP) party from the early 2000s until it merged into the National Liberal Party (PNL) in 2008.","image":"Emil Constantinescu.jpg","birth":1939.8821917808218,"death":null},"Q440970":{"label":"Crin Antonescu","description":"Romanian politician","enwiki":"Crin Antonescu","summary":"George Crin Laurențiu Antonescu (Romanian: [ˈdʒe̯ordʒe ˈkrin la.uˈrentsju antoˈnesku]; born 21 September 1959) is a Romanian politician and history teacher who served as Acting President of Romania in 2012. He was also candidate in the 2009 and 2025 presidential elections, finishing both in third place.\nBefore and after his brief acting presidency, Antonescu was Member of the Chamber of Deputies from 1992 to 2008, Minister of Youth and Sport from 1997 to 2000, Member of the Senate from 2008 to 2016, President of the National Liberal Party (PNL) from 2009 to 2014, as well as President of the Senate from 2012 to 2014.\nAntonescu became Acting President of Romania on 10 July 2012, a few days after President Traian Băsescu was suspended for a second time, and ended his term on 27 August the same year.","image":"Crin Antonescu - 28 March 2025.jpg","birth":1959.7205479452055,"death":null},"Q57279":{"label":"Traian Băsescu","description":"President of Romania from 2004 to 2014","enwiki":"Traian Băsescu","summary":"Traian Băsescu (born 4 November 1951) is a Romanian politician who served as the president of Romania from 2004 to 2014. Prior to his presidency, Băsescu served as Romanian minister of transport on multiple occasions between 1991 and 2000, and as Mayor of Bucharest from 2000 to 2004. Additionally, he was elected as leader of the Democratic Party (PD) in 2001.\nDuring his term as leader of the PD, the party formed the Justice and Truth Alliance (DA) with the National Liberal Party (PNL).","image":"Traian Băsescu (EPP Summit 2008).jpg","birth":1951.841095890411,"death":null},"Q701484":{"label":"Klaus Iohannis","description":"President of Romania from 2014 to 2025","enwiki":"Klaus Iohannis","summary":"Klaus Werner Iohannis (born 13 June 1959) is a Romanian politician, physicist, and former teacher who served as the president of Romania from 2014 until his resignation in 2025. Prior to entering national politics, Iohannis was a physics teacher at the Samuel von Brukenthal National College in his native Sibiu where he eventually served as mayor from 2000 to 2014 before ascending to the presidency.\nIohannis was first elected the mayor of the Romanian town of Sibiu in 2000, on behalf of the Democratic Forum of Germans in Romania (FDGR/DFDR). Although the Transylvanian Saxon population of Sibiu had declined to a tiny minority by the early 2000s, he won a surprise victory and was re-elected by landslides in 2004, 2008, and 2012.","image":"Klaus Iohannis - Feb 2024.jpg","birth":1959.4465753424658,"death":null},"Q80504":{"label":"Nicolae Ceaușescu","description":"dictator of Romania from 1965 to 1989","enwiki":"Nicolae Ceaușescu","summary":"Nicolae Ceaușescu (8 February [O.S. 26 January] 1918 – 25 December 1989) was a Romanian politician who was the second and last leader of Communist Romania as general secretary of the Romanian Communist Party from 1965 until his overthrow and execution in 1989. He was also the country's head of state from 1967 to 1989, serving as President of the State Council from 1967 to 1974 and as President of the Republic from 1974 until 1989.\nBorn in Scornicești, Ceaușescu joined the Romanian Communist Party in his teens and was repeatedly imprisoned under the pre-war and wartime regimes for his communist activism. After World War II, he rose through the party ranks under Gheorghe Gheorghiu-Dej, the country’s leader, whom he succeeded as general secretary.","image":"Nicolae Ceaușescu.jpg","birth":1918.0684931506848,"death":1989.9808219178083},"Q294832":{"label":"Alexandru Ioan Cuza","description":"first ruler of the United Principalities of Moldavia and Wallachia","enwiki":"Alexandru Ioan Cuza","summary":"Alexandru Ioan Cuza (Romanian: [alekˈsandru iˈo̯aŋ ˈkuza] , or Alexandru Ioan I, also Anglicised as Alexander John Cuza; 20 March [O.S. 8 March] 1820 – 15 May 1873) was the first domnitor (prince) of the Romanian Principalities through his double election as Prince of Moldavia on 5 January 1859 and Prince of Wallachia on 24 January 1859, which resulted in the unification of the two states. He was a prominent figure of the Moldavian Revolution of 1848. Following his double election, he initiated a series of liberal and progressive reforms that contributed to the modernization of Romanian society and of state structures.\nAs ruler of the Romanian Principalities, he supported a political and diplomatic activity for the recognition of the union of Moldavia and Wallachia by the suzerain Ottoman Empire and achieved constitutional and administrative unity between Moldavia and Wallachia in 1862, when the Romanian Principalities officially adopted the name Romanian United Principalities with a single capital at Bucharest, a single national assembly and a single government.","image":"Alexandru Ioan Cuza colorizat.jpg","birth":1820.2158469945355,"death":1873.3342465753424},"Q57146":{"label":"Carol I of Romania","description":"king of Romania from 1881 to 1914","enwiki":"Carol I of Romania","summary":"Carol I or Charles I of Romania (Romanian: Carol I; Romanian: [ˈkarol ɨnˈtɨj] , born Karl Eitel Friedrich Zephyrinus Ludwig von Hohenzollern-Sigmaringen; 20 April 1839 – 10 October [O.S. 27 September] 1914), nicknamed the King of Independence (Romanian: Regele Independenței; Romanian: [ˈre.d͡ʒe.le in.de.penˈden.t͡sej] ); was the monarch of Romania from 1866 to his death in 1914, ruling as Prince (Domnitor) from 1866 to 1881, and as King from 1881 to 1914. He was elected Prince of the Romanian United Principalities on 20 April 1866 after the overthrow of Alexandru Ioan Cuza by a palace coup d'état. In May 1877, Romania was proclaimed an independent and sovereign nation. The defeat of the Ottoman Empire (1878) in the Russo-Turkish War secured Romanian independence, and he was proclaimed King on 26 March [O.S. 14 March] 1881.","image":"Carol I of Romania edited.jpg","birth":1839.2986301369863,"death":1914.772602739726}},"media":{"Flag of the United Principalities of Wallachia and Moldavia (1859 - 1862).svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Flag_of_the_United_Principalities_of_Wallachia_and_Moldavia_%281859_-_1862%29.svg/330px-Flag_of_the_United_Principalities_of_Wallachia_and_Moldavia_%281859_-_1862%29.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_United_Principalities_of_Wallachia_and_Moldavia_(1859_-_1862).svg","author":"Alex:D","license":"Public domain"},"Principality of Romania - 1872 CoA.svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Principality_of_Romania_-_1872_CoA.svg/330px-Principality_of_Romania_-_1872_CoA.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Principality_of_Romania_-_1872_CoA.svg","author":"Alex Daneş; lions based on a work by Răzvan Pala.","license":"Public domain"},"Flag of Romania.svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Flag_of_Romania.svg/330px-Flag_of_Romania.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Romania.svg","author":"AdiJapan","license":"Public domain"},"Kingdom of Romania - Big CoA.svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/Kingdom_of_Romania_-_Big_CoA.svg/330px-Kingdom_of_Romania_-_Big_CoA.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kingdom_of_Romania_-_Big_CoA.svg","author":"Răzvan Pala, with suggestions and corrections by Alex Daneş","license":"CC BY 3.0"},"Coat of arms of Romania.svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/Coat_of_arms_of_Romania.svg/330px-Coat_of_arms_of_Romania.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_arms_of_Romania.svg","author":null,"license":"Public domain"},"Nicusor Dan in 2026.jpg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Nicusor_Dan_in_2026.jpg/330px-Nicusor_Dan_in_2026.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nicusor_Dan_in_2026.jpg","author":"U.S. Embassy Romania","license":"Public domain"},"First press conference of PM Luca Niculescu (cropped).jpg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/First_press_conference_of_PM_Luca_Niculescu_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-First_press_conference_of_PM_Luca_Niculescu_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:First_press_conference_of_PM_Luca_Niculescu_(cropped).jpg","author":"gov.ro (Romanian Government)","license":"Attribution"},"Ilie Bolojan (16 April 2026) (cropped).jpg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Ilie_Bolojan_%2816_April_2026%29_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Ilie_Bolojan_%2816_April_2026%29_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaig